      </article>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Two stages of competition</p>
    <p>
      The first stage is the entry filter: a crowded pool of freshers, judged mostly on proof. The second is a separate climb later, from analyst to the person who owns a domain and is trusted with decisions. Clearing the first does not clear the second, and the move into product roles is a third, separate filter.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>An ownership path exists.</strong> Once a sector trusts your work, you can sell process and requirements work to businesses directly. It scales because you set the price, repeat work with the same clients and can later bring in other analysts.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    A closer fit may exist if numbers are what you enjoy. IIBA's own list of titles for people doing business analysis includes business systems analyst and data analyst, and both lean harder on SQL. The <a href="/blog/skills/business-analyst-vs-data-analyst-career-india/">business analyst vs data analyst</a> comparison explains the trade.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sales">Basic sales and marketing for a business analyst who consults or freelances</h2>

  <p>
    In a job, your employer pays and your interviewer finds and trusts you through your proof and your communication. For consulting or freelance work, who pays changes: a small business owner, a startup, a product team short on capacity, or a larger firm that hires help. They find you through people they know, through case notes they can read, and through your past managers. The first sales skill to learn is positioning. Keep it simple, and promise no income.
  </p>

